The context report confirms the same three findings and adds two planning constraints: there are no likely tests and no related files. The fixture does have a Tailwind token source (`brand`, `surface`, `ink`), so the cleanest proposal is a semantic, token-backed Tailwind refactor with an explicit action control and fluid grid sizing.

`crimes context` confirms three findings, no related files, and no existing tests. I’d refactor [Card.tsx](/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/07-stress-frontend/src/Card.tsx:8) toward this shape:

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate: () => void;
};

export function Card({ children, onActivate }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article className="grid w-full max-w-5xl grid-cols-1 gap-6 border border-ink/20 bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-sm sm:grid-cols-4">
      <div className="min-w-0 text-lg sm:col-span-3">{children}</div>

      <aside className="text-sm text-ink/70">
        <p>Sidebar</p>
        <button
          type="button"
          className="mt-4 rounded bg-brand px-4 py-2 text-surface focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-brand"
          onClick={onActivate}
        >
          Open card
        </button>
      </aside>
    </article>
  );
}
```

How each change addresses its finding:

- `design_token_escape`: removes all inline hex, RGB, shadow, spacing, and sizing literals. It uses the existing `brand`, `surface`, and `ink` Tailwind tokens plus standard spacing, typography, and shadow utilities. If distinct border or muted colors are required, add semantic tokens to `tailwind.config.ts` rather than reintroducing literals in JSX.
- `accessible_interaction_risk`: moves the interaction from `<div onClick>` to a real `<button type="button">` with a visible accessible name. This supplies keyboard behavior, focusability, and button semantics without manually recreating them with `role`, `tabIndex`, and key handlers.
- `responsive_fragility`: replaces `800px`, `720px`, `240px`, and the fixed height with `w-full`, a tokenized maximum width, and a one-column-to-four-column responsive grid. Typography uses Tailwind’s scalable `text-lg`/`text-sm` tokens instead of raw pixel sizes.

A dedicated button is preferable to turning the entire card into a button because `children: React.ReactNode` could contain links or other interactive controls; nesting those inside a button would create another accessibility problem. Since context found no tests, I’d add a small interaction test covering keyboard activation and a narrow-viewport layout check before implementing.
